  "text": "I can't verify the answer here: I have no search or code tools, so this rests on my recollection of the literature and on checking the solutions' reasoning.\n\n**Solution 3 (12)** is unreliable. It assumes every quasivariety of Sugihara algebras is a variety, but the quasivariety lattice is strictly larger than the variety lattice. Its odd/even splitting and its claim that $\\mathsf{V}_{p,q}$ has AP iff both parts do are asserted without proof.\n\n**Solution 2 (5)** says the quasivariety lattice is countably infinite. I believe it is actually of continuum cardinality, so this is a factual error. Its table also lists the AP status of quasivarieties without showing any analysis. Still, the count of 5 matches my faint recollection of a published result on AP in Sugihara quasivarieties, though I can't confirm the exact figure.\n\n**Solution 1 (8)** gives no concrete classification. It names the trivial quasivariety, some varieties and quasivarieties \"at critical parity boundaries\", and $\\mathsf{SA}$ itself, without identifying them.\n\nMy best guess is that 5 is correct, but my confidence is low, and Solution 2's reasoning contains a clear error.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
